THOUGHT: The @Initializer read() must ensure non-null initialization of particles, particleChannels, and boundingBox. particles and particleChannels are normally set in init()/allocateChannels, and boundingBox is lazily created in getBoundingBox(). To satisfy NullAway without changing behavior, I can (1) initialize particleChannels and boundingBox in the no-arg constructor so they’re always non-null, and (2) in read(), after deserializing emitter/influencers/renderer, call allocateChannels(emitter.maxParticleCount) to ensure particles is initialized. Then I’ll run the NullAway build to confirm the error count drops and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/libgdx && sed -i '78,83s/^\(  public ParticleController()\s*{\)$/\1
    particleChannels = new ParticleChannels();
    boundingBox = new BoundingBox();/' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/graphics/g3d/particles/ParticleController.java && sed -i '368,373s/^  public void read(Json json, JsonValue jsonMap) {
    name = json.readValue("name", String.class, jsonMap);
    emitter = json.readValue("emitter", Emitter.class, jsonMap);
    influencers.addAll(json.readValue("influencers", Array.class, Influencer.class, jsonMap));
    renderer = json.readValue("renderer", ParticleControllerRenderer.class, jsonMap);
  }/  public void read(Json json, JsonValue jsonMap) {
    name = json.readValue("name", String.class, jsonMap);
    emitter = json.readValue("emitter", Emitter.class, jsonMap);
    influencers.addAll(json.readValue("influencers", Array.class, Influencer.class, jsonMap));
    renderer = json.readValue("renderer", ParticleControllerRenderer.class, jsonMap);
    allocateChannels(emitter.maxParticleCount);
  }/' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/graphics/g3d/particles/ParticleController.java && ./gradlew gdx:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -n "METHOD_NO_INIT" new_build_output.log || tail -n 40 new_build_output.log```

